Rem ===================================================================
Rem mungo-caverns-turn.shoddy -- what one command does.
Rem
Rem Everything between a line of input and the game it leaves behind:
Rem the five rewrites the parser applies before dispatch, the verb and
Rem object dispatch itself, moving, what happens after a move -- the
Rem curmudgeons' turn, the pit, dying and being put back together -- and
Rem the small housekeeping the prompt does on arrival.
Rem
Rem Command(g, line) -> Done is a function: a Game and a line in, a Game
Rem and a Phase out, with everything the rules said carried on the Game's
Rem own output channel. Nothing here prompts and nothing here prints, so
Rem test.shoddy can drive a whole turn headless. The one exception is
Rem Croak2, which offers you a resurrection and must read the answer
Rem between two writes; it is one of the ten question sites the port
Rem admits to.
Rem
Rem The turn loop that calls all this -- the prompt, the flush, and the
Rem decision to go round again -- is in mungo-caverns.shoddy.
Rem ===================================================================


Rem Moved says whether the turn completed a move, which
Rem is what sends the loop back round to describe the room again. Only a
Rem motion does that -- a verb returns GoClearObj and stays put, which
Rem is why "get lamp" does not reprint the room.
Rem A curmudgeon that has seen you and is standing where you
Rem meant to go bars the way. It is checked against the curmudgeon's *previous*
Rem room, because the curmudgeons have not moved yet this turn.
Def CurmudgeonBars(g As Game, dest As Number, i As Number, n As Number) As Boolean
    If i > n Then
        False
    Else
        Let d = Nth(Curmudgeons(g), i)
        If DOld(d) = dest And DSeen(d) Then
            True
        Else
            CurmudgeonBars(g, dest, i + 1, n)

Def Blocked(g As Game, dest As Number) As Game
    If ClosingTime(g) And dest <> 0 And OutsideAt(dest) Then
        With(Panicked(VoicedId(g, MsgExitClosed())), PreviousLocation = Location(g))
    Else
        If (dest <> Location(g) And Not ForcedAt(Location(g))
            And Not Contains(Conds(RoomAt(g)), "NOARRR")
            And CurmudgeonBars(g, dest, 1, NCurmudgeons() - 1)) Then
            VoicedId(g, MsgCurmudgeonBlock())
        Else
            With(g, PreviousLocation = Location(g), Location = dest)

Def OutsideAt(dest As Number) As Boolean
    (Contains(Conds(Nth(Locations(), dest + 1)), "ABOVE")
     Or Contains(Conds(Nth(Locations(), dest + 1)), "FOREST"))

Rem The panic clock starts the first time the closing
Rem cave refuses to let you out, and only that first time.
Def Panicked(g As Game) As Game
    If Panic(g) Then
        g
    Else
        With(g, ClockToBlinding = PanicTime(), Panic = True)

Rem Not a count: the index of the first curmudgeon here, or
Rem zero when none is (or the curmudgeons are not yet awake), or minus one
Rem when every curmudgeon is dead. The three answers are treated differently --
Rem an ogre will not be panicked away by curmudgeons that no longer exist.
Def AtCurmudgeon(g As Game, i As Number, n As Number, anyAlive As Boolean) As Number
    If i > n Then
        AllDeadOr(anyAlive)
    Else
        Let d = Nth(Curmudgeons(g), i)
        If DLoc(d) = Location(g) Then
            i
        Else
            AtCurmudgeon(g, i + 1, n, anyAlive Or DLoc(d) <> 0)

Def AllDeadOr(anyAlive As Boolean) As Number
    If anyAlive Then
        0
    Else
        0 - 1

Def CurmudgeonsHere(g As Game) As Number
    If CurmudgeonActivation(g) < 2 Then
        0
    Else
        AtCurmudgeon(g, 1, NCurmudgeons() - 1, False)

Rem Everything that happens once the player has been
Rem put down in the new room: the curmudgeons get their turn, and then the
Rem easiest death in the game gets its chance.
Rem
Rem The pit roll is guarded by three tests before it, so the draw only
Rem happens when all three hold. Drawing unconditionally would shift the
Rem stream and break every seeded game -- the same trap as the
Rem curmudgeon activation test.
Def AfterMove(g As Game) As Game
    Let m = CurmudgeonMove(g)
    Let g1 = Result(m)
    If Not Survived(m) Then
        Croak(g1)
    Else
        If Location(g1) = 0 Then
            Rem the death pseudo-rooms all force you to
            Rem location nowhere, and arriving there is what kills you.
            Croak(g1)
        Else
            PitCheck(g1)

Def PitCheck(g As Game) As Game
    If Not ForcedAt(Location(g)) And IsDarkHere(g) And LeftRoomWasDark(g) Then
        Let fell = Pct(LcgState(g), 35)
        Let g2 = Stepped(g)
        If fell Then
            Croak(With(VoicedId(g2, MsgPitFall()), LocationBeforeLast = Location(g2)))
        Else
            g2
    Else
        g

Rem Dying offers a resurrection, and there are only three
Rem on offer. Everything you were carrying is dropped where you died --
Rem backwards through the object table, so the bird lands before the cage
Rem -- except the lamp, which is turned off and left outside where you can
Rem reach it without one.
Def Croak(g As Game) As Game
    Let n = Deaths(g)
    Let obit = Nth(Obituaries(), n + 1)
    Let g1 = With(g, Deaths = n + 1)
    If ClosingTime(g1) Then
        Rem dying during closing time is final -- it is so
        Rem close to closing time anyway that the game just calls it a day.
        With(VoicedId(g1, MsgDeathClosing()), Outcome = Died(""))
    Else
        Croak2(g1, obit)

Rem A question read between two writes: whatever the rules have said so
Rem far has to be out before the offer of resurrection goes up.
Def Croak2(gIn As Game, obit As Obit) As Game
    Let g = Flushed(gIn)
    If (YesNo(Query(obit), Yes(obit), Nth(Messages(), MsgOkMan() + 1))
        And Deaths(g) < NDeaths()) Then
        Reincarnate(g)
    Else
        Rem dying counts as seeing it through, and is worth
        Rem four points that walking away is not
        With(g, Outcome = Died(""))

Def Reincarnate(g As Game) As Game
    Let lamp = Lamp()
    Let g1 = Doused(g, lamp)
    Let g2 = Scatter(g1, Length(Objects()) - 1, 1)
    With(g2, PreviousLocation = LocNamed("LOC_BUILDING"), Location = LocNamed("LOC_BUILDING"))

Def Doused(g As Game, lamp As Number) As Game
    If Toting(g, lamp) Then
        With(g, Props = SetNth(Props(g), lamp + 1, LampDark()))
    Else
        g

Def Scatter(g As Game, i As Number, low As Number) As Game
    If i < low Then
        g
    Else
        If Toting(g, i) Then
            Scatter(Dropped(g, i), i - 1, low)
        Else
            Scatter(g, i - 1, low)

Def Dropped(g As Game, i As Number) As Game
    Let dest = FallenTo(g, i)
    With(g, Held = Filter(Held(g), Fn(x) => x <> i),
            ObjectsAt = SetNth(ObjectsAt(g), dest + 1, Prepend(i, Nth(ObjectsAt(g), dest + 1))),
            ObjectLocation = SetNth(ObjectLocation(g), i + 1, dest))

Def FallenTo(g As Game, i As Number) As Number
    If i = Lamp() Then
        LocNamed("LOC_START")
    Else
        LocationBeforeLast(g)

Def Cleared(g As Game) As Game
    Rem clearing the command is also what records the object
    Rem it referred to, which the bird hint later consults.
    With(g, PreviousObject = PendingObject(g), PendingObject = NoObject(), PendingVerb = NoVerb())

Rem The dispatch turned inside out: run the verb, then let its phase
Rem code say what survives into the next command.
Rem The verb runs on its own first; a second word becomes
Rem its subject only if the verb asks, by returning GO_WORD2. Carry asks,
Rem which is how "get knife" reaches the presence check; tame does not,
Rem which is why "calm food" never looks at the food and simply answers
Rem that it is game if you would care to explain how.
Def Verbed(g As Game, id As Number, k2 As Kind, raw2 As String, raw1 As String) As Done
    Let d = DoAction(g, id, raw2, PendingObject(g), raw1)
    If (WantsWord2(d) And Len(raw2) > 0) Then
        Shifted(With(After(d), PendingVerb = id, PendingRaw = raw1), k2, raw2)
    Else
        Resolved(d, id, raw1)

Def WantsWord2(d As Done) As Boolean
    Select Case How(d)
        Case GoWord2
            True
        Case Else
            False

Def RunVerb(g As Game, verb As Number, obj As Number, arg As String, raw As String) As Done
    Rem clearing the command records its object, so the
    Rem object must ride along for Cleared to move into PreviousObject -- the
    Rem bird hint reads it there.
    Resolved(DoAction(With(g, PendingObject = obj), verb, arg, obj, raw), verb, raw)

Def Resolved(d As Done, verb As Number, raw As String) As Done
    Select Case How(d)
        Case GoUnknown
            Rem the verb is remembered, the object is not
            Done(With(Voiced(After(d), Render(Nth(Messages(), MsgDoWhat() + 1), { Capitalize(raw) })),
                      PendingObject = NoObject(), PendingVerb = verb, PendingRaw = raw), GoKeep())
        Case GoTop
            Done(Cleared(After(d)), GoTop())
        Case GoMove
            Done(Cleared(AfterMove(After(d))), GoTop())
        Case GoWord2
            Rem the second word becomes a command in its own right
            Done(Cleared(After(d)), GoKeep())
        Case Else
            Done(Cleared(After(d)), GoKeep())

Rem Five small rewrites the parser applies before the
Rem command is dispatched. Two matter a great deal: an object followed by
Rem a verb is swapped into verb-object order, so "lamp get" works; and
Rem "water plant" becomes "pour water", which is how the beanstalk gets
Rem grown, since neither word on its own is a verb.
Type Said
    Word1   As Kind
    Word2   As Kind
    RawWord As String
    RawTwo  As String

Def Preprocess(g As Game, ts As List) As Said
    Let k1 = ClassifyOld(RawAt(ts, 1, OldStyleMode()), MagicWord(g), OldStyleMode())
    Let k2 = ClassifyOld(RawAt(ts, 2, OldStyleMode()), MagicWord(g), OldStyleMode())
    If Not IsObjectWord(k1) Then
        Said(k1, k2, RawAt(ts, 1, OldStyleMode()), RawAt(ts, 2, OldStyleMode()))
    Else
        If IsActionWord(k2) Then
            Rem object-then-verb: say it the other way round
            Said(k2, k1, RawAt(ts, 2, OldStyleMode()), RawAt(ts, 1, OldStyleMode()))
        Else
            Caging(g, k1, k2, ts)

Rem "cage bird" is not a phrase the grammar knows, so
Rem it is rewritten into a plain "carry bird".
Def Caging(g As Game, k1 As Kind, k2 As Kind, ts As List) As Said
    Let a = ObjectOf2(k1)
    Let b = ObjectOf2(k2)
    If (a = ObjNamed("CAGE") And b = ObjNamed("BIRD")
        And Here(g, a) And Here(g, b)) Then
        Said(KAction(ActionNamed("CARRY")), k2, "carry", RawAt(ts, 2, OldStyleMode()))
    Else
        Grating(g, k1, k2, ts)

Rem Saying "grate" is asking to go to it, and which
Rem grate you mean depends on which side of it you are standing.
Def Grating(g As Game, k1 As Kind, k2 As Kind, ts As List) As Said
    If ObjectOf2(k1) <> ObjNamed("GRATE") Then
        Watering(g, k1, k2, ts)
    Else
        Said(KMotion(GrateWay(g)), k2, RawAt(ts, 1, OldStyleMode()), RawAt(ts, 2, OldStyleMode()))

Def GrateWay(g As Game) As Number
    If InAny(g, { "LOC_START", "LOC_VALLEY", "LOC_SLIT" }, 1, 3) Then
        MotionNamed("DEPRESSION")
    Else
        If InAny(g, { "LOC_COBBLE", "LOC_DEBRIS", "LOC_AWKWARD",
                      "LOC_BIRDCHAMBER", "LOC_PITTOP" }, 1, 5) Then
            MotionNamed("ENTRANCE")
        Else
            MotionNamed("GRATE")

Def InAny(g As Game, tags As List, i As Number, n As Number) As Boolean
    If i > n Then
        False
    Else
        If Location(g) = LocNamed(Nth(tags, i)) Then
            True
        Else
            InAny(g, tags, i + 1, n)

Rem "water plant", "oil door" and their kin become a POUR of the liquid,
Rem but only when the thing named is actually here.
Def Watering(g As Game, k1 As Kind, k2 As Kind, ts As List) As Said
    Let a = ObjectOf2(k1)
    Let b = ObjectOf2(k2)
    If (Not IsLiquid(a) Or Not IsPourable(b) Or Not At(g, b)) Then
        Said(k1, k2, RawAt(ts, 1, OldStyleMode()), RawAt(ts, 2, OldStyleMode()))
    Else
        Said(KAction(ActionNamed("POUR")), KObject(a), "pour", RawAt(ts, 1, OldStyleMode()))

Def IsLiquid(i As Number) As Boolean
    i <> NoObject() And (i = ObjNamed("WATER") Or i = ObjNamed("OIL"))

Def IsPourable(i As Number) As Boolean
    i <> NoObject() And (i = ObjNamed("PLANT") Or i = ObjNamed("DOOR"))

Def ObjectOf2(k As Kind) As Number
    Select Case k
        Case KObject(id)
            id
        Case Else
            NoObject()

Def IsObjectWord(k As Kind) As Boolean
    Select Case k
        Case KObject(id)
            True
        Case Else
            False

Def IsActionWord(k As Kind) As Boolean
    Select Case k
        Case KAction(id)
            True
        Case Else
            False

Rem After ten spellings-out the game suggests the
    Rem shorter form. Both counters are per-game, not per-room.
Rem The nudge runs on every word the processing loop sees, and the
Rem GoWord2 shift feeds the second word back through it -- so "go west"
Rem bumps both counters, go first and west second.
Def Nudged(g As Game, ts As List) As Game
    Let w1 = Lower(RawAt(ts, 1, OldStyleMode()))
    Let w2 = Lower(RawAt(ts, 2, OldStyleMode()))
    NudgedWest(NudgedGo(g, w1, w2), w1, w2)

Def NudgedGo(g As Game, w1 As String, w2 As String) As Game
    If w1 = "go" And Len(w2) > 0 Then
        NudgeGo(With(g, GoCount = GoCount(g) + 1))
    Else
        g

Def NudgedWest(g As Game, w1 As String, w2 As String) As Game
    If w1 = "west" Or (w2 = "west" And w1 <> "say") Then
        NudgeWest(With(g, WestCount = WestCount(g) + 1))
    Else
        g

Def NudgeWest(g As Game) As Game
    If WestCount(g) = 10 Then
        VoicedId(g, MsgWIsWest())
    Else
        g

Def NudgeGo(g As Game) As Game
    If GoCount(g) = 10 Then
        VoicedId(g, MsgGoUnneeded())
    Else
        g

Def Command(gIn As Game, line As String) As Done
    Let ts = Tokens(line)
    Let g = Nudged(gIn, ts)
    If EnteringWater(g, ts) Then
        Rem "enter water" is answered on the spot and the command thrown
        Rem away without processing either word further.
        Done(VoicedId(g, WaterEntryReply(g)), GoKeep())
    Else
        Command2(g, Preprocess(g, ts))

Def EnteringWater(g As Game, ts As List) As Boolean
    Let k1 = ClassifyOld(RawAt(ts, 1, OldStyleMode()), MagicWord(g), OldStyleMode())
    Let k2 = ClassifyOld(RawAt(ts, 2, OldStyleMode()), MagicWord(g), OldStyleMode())
    (IsMotionKind(k1, "ENTER")
     And (IsMotionKind(k2, "STREAM") Or ObjectOf2(k2) = ObjNamed("WATER")))

Def IsMotionKind(k As Kind, t As String) As Boolean
    Select Case k
        Case KMotion(id)
            Tag(Nth(Motions(), id + 1)) = t
        Case Else
            False

Def WaterEntryReply(g As Game) As Number
    If LiqLoc(g) = ObjNamed("WATER") Then
        MsgFeetWet()
    Else
        MsgWhereQuery()

Def Command2(g As Game, said As Said) As Done
    Let k1 = Word1(said)
    Let k2 = Word2(said)
    Let raw1 = RawWord(said)
    Select Case k1
        Case KMotion(id)
            Done(Cleared(AfterMove(Move(g, id))), GoTop())
        Case KMagic
            Rem the reservoir word is a command in its own right
            Resolved(Reservoir(g), NoVerb(), raw1)
        Case KObject(id)
            ObjectWord(g, id, raw1)
        Case KAction(id)
            Rem A verb with a second word does not run
            Rem straight away: it remembers itself and hands the second
            Rem word back to the parser as a command in its own right.
            Rem That detour is what makes "get knife" reach the
            Rem object-presence check below.
            Rem The deferral is a blanket rule, not a per-verb one:
            Rem any verb with a second
            Rem word -- any verb but say -- returns GoWord2 at once.
            Rem Say is the
            Rem single exception, which is why its guard lives in Shifted.
            Rem And a noaction verb speaks its message and is
            Rem done with it, before the GoWord2 rule below ever runs. So
            Rem "no rub" answers for `no` and never looks at `rub`.
            If IsNumberWord(k2) Or NoAction(Nth(Actions(), id + 1)) Then
                RunVerb(g, id, PendingObject(g), RawTwo(said), raw1)
            Else
                If Len(RawTwo(said)) = 0 Then
                    RunVerb(g, id, PendingObject(g), "", raw1)
                Else
                    Shifted(With(g, PendingVerb = id, PendingRaw = raw1), k2, RawTwo(said))
        Case Else
            Rem a wordless command is treated as an empty
            Rem motion, and playermove's default answer for it is CANT_APPLY.
            If Len(raw1) = 0 Then
                Done(Cleared(AfterMove(VoicedId(g, MsgCantApply()))), GoTop())
            Else
                Done(Cleared(Voiced(g, Render(Nth(Messages(), MsgDontKnow() + 1), { raw1 }))), GoKeep())

Rem Most verbs let the second word be re-parsed on its
Rem own -- that detour is what makes "get knife" reach the presence check.
Rem Say is the exception: it reads word[1] and echoes it whatever it is, so
Rem "say axe" answers Okay, "axe" rather than going looking for an axe. The
Rem magic words still reach the shift, because say hands those back itself.
Def Shifted(g As Game, k As Kind, raw As String) As Done
    If (PendingVerb(g) = ActionNamed("SAY") And Not SayObeys(k)) Then
        Done(Cleared(Voiced(g, Render(Nth(Messages(), MsgOkeyDokey() + 1), { raw }))), GoKeep())
    Else
        Shifted2(g, k, raw)

Rem The magic word parts the reservoir -- but only
Rem where the reservoir is; anywhere else nothing happens at all. Said from
Rem the far shore it works and strands you in the dark, which is fatal: the
Rem waters close over the way you came.
Def Reservoir(g As Game) As Done
    Let reser = ObjNamed("RESER")
    If (Not At(g, reser) And Location(g) <> LocNamed("LOC_RESBOTTOM")) Then
        Done(VoicedId(g, MsgNothingHappens()), GoClearObj())
    Else
        Let g1 = StateChange(g, reser, Parted(PropOf(g, reser)))
        If At(g1, reser) Then
            Done(g1, GoClearObj())
        Else
            Done(Croak(With(VoicedId(g1, MsgNotBright()), LocationBeforeLast = Location(g1))), GoClearObj())

Def Parted(prop As Number) As Number
    If prop = 1 Then
        0
    Else
        1

Rem The words say hands on rather than echoes: the three transport words,
Rem the giant's fee-fie-foe-foo, and the reservoir's own magic word.
Def SayObeys(k As Kind) As Boolean
    Select Case k
        Case KMagic
            True
        Case KMotion(id)
            (Tag(Nth(Motions(), id + 1)) = "XYZZY"
             Or Tag(Nth(Motions(), id + 1)) = "PLUGH"
             Or Tag(Nth(Motions(), id + 1)) = "PLOVER")
        Case KAction(id)
            BigWord(Nth(Actions(), id + 1))
        Case Else
            False

Def Shifted2(g As Game, k As Kind, raw As String) As Done
    Select Case k
        Case KObject(id)
            ObjectWord(g, id, raw)
        Case KMagic
            Resolved(Reservoir(g), NoVerb(), raw)
        Case KMotion(id)
            Done(Cleared(AfterMove(Move(g, id))), GoTop())
        Case KAction(id)
            RunVerb(g, id, PendingObject(g), "", raw)
        Case Else
            Done(Cleared(Voiced(g, Render(Nth(Messages(), MsgDontKnow() + 1), { raw }))), GoKeep())

Rem Before anything is done with an object, it has
Rem to be here -- or answer to one of the aliases accepted in order:
Rem a prowling curmudgeon, the liquid in the bottle or the room (never once
Rem the cave is closed), the oil in the urn, the taller plant, the rod
Rem that is really the other rod. The knife is the odd one out: naming
Rem it where the curmudgeons threw it is how you learn it has gone.
Def ObjectWord(g As Game, id As Number, raw As String) As Done
    Select Case True
        Case Here(g, id) Or At(g, id)
            KObjectSaid(g, id, raw)
        Case id = ObjNamed("CURMUDGEON") And CurmudgeonsHere(g) > 0
            KObjectSaid(g, id, raw)
        Case (Not Closed(g)
              And ((Liquid(g) = id And Here(g, ObjNamed("BOTTLE")))
                   Or (id = LiqLoc(g) And id <> NoObject())))
            KObjectSaid(g, id, raw)
        Case (id = ObjNamed("OIL") And Here(g, ObjNamed("URN"))
              And PropOf(g, ObjNamed("URN")) <> 0)
            KObjectSaid(g, ObjNamed("URN"), raw)
        Case (id = ObjNamed("PLANT") And At(g, ObjNamed("PLANT2"))
              And PropOf(g, ObjNamed("PLANT2")) <> 0)
            KObjectSaid(g, ObjNamed("PLANT2"), raw)
        Case Else
            ObjectWord2(g, id, raw)

Def ObjectWord2(g As Game, id As Number, raw As String) As Done
    Select Case True
        Case id = ObjNamed("KNIFE") And KnifeLocation(g) = Location(g)
            Done(Cleared(With(VoicedId(g, MsgKnivesVanish()), KnifeLocation = 0 - 1)), GoKeep())
        Case id = ObjNamed("ROD") And Here(g, ObjNamed("ROD2"))
            KObjectSaid(g, ObjNamed("ROD2"), raw)
        Case FindsAnything(g, id)
            KObjectSaid(g, id, raw)
        Case Else
            Done(Cleared(Voiced(g, Render(Nth(Messages(), MsgNoSee() + 1), { raw }))), GoKeep())

Rem FIND and INVENTORY are allowed to ask after things that are not here;
Rem that is what they are for.
Def FindsAnything(g As Game, id As Number) As Boolean
    If PendingVerb(g) = NoVerb() Then
        False
    Else
        Let verbTag = Tag(Nth(Actions(), PendingVerb(g) + 1))
        verbTag = "FIND" Or verbTag = "INVENTORY"

Def IsNumberWord(k As Kind) As Boolean
    Select Case k
        Case KNumeric(v)
            True
        Case Else
            False

Rem A bare object name runs the verb left over from
Rem last turn, if there was one; otherwise the game remembers the object
Rem and asks what you meant by it.
Def KObjectSaid(g As Game, id As Number, raw1 As String) As Done
    If PendingVerb(g) = NoVerb() Then
        Done(With(Voiced(g, Render(Nth(Messages(), MsgWhatDo() + 1), { raw1 })), PendingObject = id), GoKeep())
    Else
        Rem After the shift the remembered raw word is the object's, so a
        Rem verb that quotes it back at you names the thing you asked about
        Rem -- "I see no keys here", not "I see no read here".
        RunVerb(g, PendingVerb(g), id, "", raw1)

Rem An object named in the same breath wins; otherwise the one left over
Rem from the previous command stands in.
Def SecondObject(k As Kind, pend As Number) As Number
    Select Case k
        Case KObject(id)
            id
        Case Else
            pend

Rem While the cave is closed, anything you are toting
Rem that is still stashed flips out of its pile at the next prompt, so
Rem the repository piles get described only once picked up and put down.
Rem The oyster hints at its engraving on the prompt you first hold it.
Rem A knife left where a curmudgeon threw it vanishes for
Rem good the moment you take a prompt anywhere else.
Def KnifeGone(g As Game) As Game
    If KnifeLocation(g) > 0 And KnifeLocation(g) <> Location(g) Then
        With(g, KnifeLocation = 0)
    Else
        g

Def KnifeMarked(g As Game) As Game
    If KnifeLocation(g) >= 0 Then
        With(g, KnifeLocation = Location(g))
    Else
        g

Def Unstashed(g As Game) As Game
    If Not Closed(g) Then
        g
    Else
        Let g1 = OysterClue(g)
        UnstashHeld(g1, Held(g1), 1, Length(Held(g1)))

Def OysterClue(g As Game) As Game
    Let oyster = ObjNamed("OYSTER")
    If Toting(g, oyster) And IsHidden(g, oyster) Then
        Voiced(g, Nth(Descs(ObjAt(oyster)), 2))
    Else
        g

Def UnstashHeld(g As Game, hs As List, i As Number, n As Number) As Game
    If i > n Then
        g
    Else
        Rem Revealed leaves a thing that was not stashed exactly as it was,
        Rem so one call serves stashed and unstashed alike.
        UnstashHeld(Revealed(g, Nth(hs, i)), hs, i + 1, n)

Rem The visit is counted with the object listing, under the same
Rem guard that skips the listing in the dark. So stumbling through a room
Rem you cannot see does not count as having seen it, and lighting the lamp
Rem still earns you the long description.
Rem show is true on arrival in a room, which is also when the visit
Rem counter ticks -- both belong with the object listing.
Def Shown(g As Game, show As Boolean) As Game
    If show Then
        Let g1 = Describe(g)
        If IsDarkHere(g1) Then
            g1
        Else
            Visited(g1)
    Else
        g
